!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/class2/schema/valid/an-enum-declares-which-kind-of-enumeration-it-is-expected.tn"
!!schema:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/schema-sidecar.tn"
{
  spec: "[TSON-SCHEMA] §7.4, [TSON-SCHEMA] §9"
  description: """
    An enum models two things and `profile` says which. `status` is a vocabulary: its members are
    names, so they stay a named scope for [TSON-DATA] §8.2's hygiene and each has a host-safe
    spelling. `activity` is a value set: two strings a document may carry, neither of them a name and
    neither writable without quotes.

    Both are enums, and the member set is typed by `text` in each. What separates them is the
    declaration, never the shape of the members -- a resolver inferring the kind from how the members
    happen to be spelled would switch a spoofing check on and off by accident.

    `IDENTIFIER` is the default, so `status` says nothing and resolves exactly as it did before the
    facet existed; a default concrete in the constructor's own declaration is omitted from the
    binding record (§5.6), which is why only `activity` states one.
    """
  meta: "meta.tn"
  import: ["core.tn"]
  valid: {
    resolved: """
      !schema {
        status => !type_definition {
          source: enum
          body: !enum { members: [OPEN ACTIVE DONE] }
        }

        activity => !type_definition {
          source: enum
          body: !enum { members: ["sedentary" "lightly active"]  profile: TEXT }
        }
      }
      """
  }
}
